import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a3 as n}from"./chunks/framework.Dz5Q8p4s.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Long Video Understanding","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Long Video Understanding","outline":[2,2]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/tutorials/long-video-understanding.md","filePath":"en/tutorials/long-video-understanding.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"en/tutorials/long-video-understanding.md"};function i(s,e,l,h,d,p){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="long-video-understanding" tabindex="-1">Long Video Understanding <a class="header-anchor" href="#long-video-understanding" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Long Video Understanding&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p><strong>Full text (Chinese)</strong>: <a href="/notes/tutorials/long-video-understanding">长视频理解</a><strong>Coverage</strong>: June 2025 – August 2026, with pre-2025 work included only as background. <strong>Scale</strong>: 13 chapters (0–12) + 4 appendices, <strong>1546 papers, every one of them broken down individually</strong> in Chapters 11–12.</p></blockquote><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">About this English page</p><p>The tutorial itself is written in Chinese. This page is its English companion: it states what the tutorial argues, reproduces the measurements that the tutorial treats as its load-bearing evidence, and gives the full chapter structure. Every derivation, table, diagram and per-paper entry lives in the <a href="/notes/tutorials/long-video-understanding">Chinese edition</a>.</p></div><h2 id="who-this-is-for" tabindex="-1">Who this is for <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-this-is-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who this is for&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Anyone who can already make a multimodal model answer questions about a ten-second clip, and now has to make it work on an hour of video — a lecture recording, a surveillance feed, a full surgical procedure, a day of egocentric footage from smart glasses. The tutorial assumes you know what a ViT and an LLM are, and nothing else about video.</p><p>It is deliberately not a survey. It is organised around <strong>why the obvious approach fails</strong>, and it is unusually insistent that most reported progress in this area is not measuring what it claims to measure.</p><h2 id="what-this-tutorial-argues" tabindex="-1">What this tutorial argues <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-tutorial-argues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this tutorial argues&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="four-walls-chapter-1" tabindex="-1">Four walls (Chapter 1) <a class="header-anchor" href="#four-walls-chapter-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Four walls (Chapter 1)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Long video understanding is not one problem but four failure modes that arrive together:</p><ol><li><strong>The token wall.</strong> One hour at 1 FPS is 3600 frames; at ~576 tokens per frame that is ~2.07M visual tokens. Attention is quadratic in sequence length, so this rules out nearly every model before any question is asked.</li><li><strong>The evidence-sparsity wall.</strong> The answer usually lives in a handful of seconds. Everything else is not merely useless — it actively dilutes the evidence and drives the model back onto its language prior.</li><li><strong>The evaluation wall.</strong> The one the tutorial pushes hardest. Benchmarks routinely reward behaviour that has nothing to do with watching the video.</li><li><strong>The no-rewind wall.</strong> In streaming settings you cannot look back, so state must be bounded and written before you know what will be asked.</li></ol><h3 id="ten-families-of-methods-one-decision-tree-chapter-7" tabindex="-1">Ten families of methods, one decision tree (Chapter 7) <a class="header-anchor" href="#ten-families-of-methods-one-decision-tree-chapter-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ten families of methods, one decision tree (Chapter 7)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Long-context extension, visual-token compression, frame/clip selection, memory, retrieval augmentation (Video-RAG), agentic tool use, RL and post-training, streaming, architectural changes (SSM / linear attention / decode acceleration), and modality extension (subtitles, audio, on-screen text, space). Chapter 7.0 frames all ten as the same object — an <em>evidence-acquisition operator</em> feeding a <em>reader</em> — and gives a cost/benefit table plus an explicit &quot;when not to use this&quot; section for every family.</p><h3 id="evaluation-traps-chapter-5-4" tabindex="-1">Evaluation traps (Chapter 5.4) <a class="header-anchor" href="#evaluation-traps-chapter-5-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Evaluation traps (Chapter 5.4)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Ten shortcuts that must be ruled out before a number means anything: language-prior leakage, single-frame solvability, losing to uniform sampling, subtitle/ASR dominance, invariance to temporal shuffling, interval-length priors, position bias, uncalibrated judges, data leakage, and single-backbone validation. Chapter 5.3.1 specifies a mandatory calibration procedure for GPT-as-judge.</p><h2 id="the-measurements-the-tutorial-leans-on" tabindex="-1">The measurements the tutorial leans on <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-measurements-the-tutorial-leans-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The measurements the tutorial leans on&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every number below is transcribed verbatim from the cited abstract; none of it was reproduced by the author.</p><p><strong>Timestamps are the weak point, and confidence does not warn you.</strong> Prompted for timestamps, strong VLMs reach as little as <strong>3.8% R@0.5 on Charades-STA</strong>, and <strong>77–80% of their wrong predictions carry low output entropy</strong> — the models are confidently wrong, and entropy-based error detection performs <em>below a random classifier</em>. The same work shows the failure lives in the task interface, not in perception: holding weights fixed and replacing timestamp generation with yes/no questioning recovers much of the ability (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08315" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2608.08315</a>).</p><p><strong>Shuffling the frames often changes nothing.</strong> On existing benchmarks, models score very well even when frames are scrambled — evidence that they were never relying on temporal order (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.08979" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2512.08979</a>). This is why the tutorial treats a shuffle ablation as a mandatory, three-line experiment rather than an optional one.</p><p><strong>A long context window is not long-video understanding.</strong> State-of-the-art long-context VLMs can ingest around 1000 frames and still fail to use that length, losing to distractors inside their own context window (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02001" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2507.02001</a>).</p><p><strong>Robustness collapses quietly.</strong> Under ordinary perturbations — motion blur, glare, occlusion — frontier video reasoning models drop <strong>15–30 percentage points</strong> on embodied benchmarks while remaining unaware that their visual evidence has degraded (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.26904" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2606.26904</a>).</p><p><strong>The ground truth for temporal grounding is a distribution, not an interval.</strong> Re-annotate the same query–video pair and independent annotators mark moments overlapping by <strong>less than half</strong> on a large fraction of samples — which is exactly the threshold R@0.5 is built on (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07434" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2608.07434</a>).</p><p><strong>Breaking a task&#39;s hidden assumption produces near-zero, not merely lower, scores.</strong> Prior state-of-the-art MLLMs optimised for one-to-one temporal grounding score close to zero when a single query legitimately corresponds to several disjoint segments (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06294" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2606.06294</a>).</p><p><strong>Accuracy gains have a latency price that is rarely reported.</strong> Neuro-symbolic long-video QA improves accuracy by grounding temporal reasoning in formal verification, at <strong>up to 90× the latency</strong> of plain VLM prompting (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23553" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2602.23553</a>).</p><h2 id="chapter-outline" tabindex="-1">Chapter outline <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-outline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter outline&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="chapter-0-—-how-to-use-this-tutorial" tabindex="-1">Chapter 0 — How to use this tutorial <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-0-—-how-to-use-this-tutorial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 0 — How to use this tutorial&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Target reader, prerequisites, what &quot;long&quot; means here, and the one-sentence spine.</p><h3 id="chapter-1-—-definition-boundaries-and-the-four-walls" tabindex="-1">Chapter 1 — Definition, boundaries, and the four walls <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-1-—-definition-boundaries-and-the-four-walls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 1 — Definition, boundaries, and the four walls&quot;">​</a></h3><p>What is in scope, the four walls above, and a wall-to-antidote table.</p><h3 id="chapter-2-—-principles-i-how-a-video-enters-a-language-model" tabindex="-1">Chapter 2 — Principles I: how a video enters a language model <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-2-—-principles-i-how-a-video-enters-a-language-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 2 — Principles I: how a video enters a language model&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The five-stage pipeline — decode/sample, visual encoding, connector, position and time encoding, LLM backbone — and a minimal working video LLM assembled from it.</p><h3 id="chapter-3-—-principles-ii-sparse-evidence-budget-recall-ceiling" tabindex="-1">Chapter 3 — Principles II: sparse evidence, budget, recall ceiling <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-3-—-principles-ii-sparse-evidence-budget-recall-ceiling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 3 — Principles II: sparse evidence, budget, recall ceiling&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Why frame selection is an <em>upper-bound</em> problem; three levels of redundancy; the query-relevance spectrum as a classification axis more useful than method names; four different meanings of &quot;budget&quot;; bounded state in streaming.</p><h3 id="chapter-4-—-task-map" tabindex="-1">Chapter 4 — Task map <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-4-—-task-map" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 4 — Task map&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Six task classes (QA, grounding, generation, retrieval, interaction, specialised) with a trivial-baseline cheat sheet for each.</p><h3 id="chapter-5-—-benchmarks-and-metrics-plus-the-evaluation-traps-chapter" tabindex="-1">Chapter 5 — Benchmarks and metrics, plus the evaluation-traps chapter <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-5-—-benchmarks-and-metrics-plus-the-evaluation-traps-chapter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 5 — Benchmarks and metrics, plus the evaluation-traps chapter&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Classical anchors, the 2025-06 → 2026-08 benchmarks grouped by the dimension they probe, metric pitfalls and substitutes, the ten traps, and a reusable evaluation checklist.</p><h3 id="chapter-6-—-data" tabindex="-1">Chapter 6 — Data <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-6-—-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 6 — Data&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Three data types across three training stages, why long-video annotation is so expensive, the mainstream corpora, new datasets by purpose, what synthetic data does and does not buy, and six practical rules.</p><h3 id="chapter-7-—-methods-the-core-chapter" tabindex="-1">Chapter 7 — Methods (the core chapter) <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-7-—-methods-the-core-chapter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 7 — Methods (the core chapter)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The ten families, each with motivation → core idea → mechanism → representative work → trade-offs → when not to use it.</p><h3 id="chapter-8-—-systems-and-engineering" tabindex="-1">Chapter 8 — Systems and engineering <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-8-—-systems-and-engineering" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 8 — Systems and engineering&quot;">​</a></h3><p>End-to-end latency breakdown, memory budget tables, parallelism, edge deployment, the multi-user cost model, and a reproducible evaluation environment.</p><h3 id="chapter-9-—-practice" tabindex="-1">Chapter 9 — Practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-9-—-practice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 9 — Practice&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Five deployment recipes (offline QA, low-latency QA, streaming assistant, video-library retrieval, leaderboard), a seven-step build guide, ten failure modes, and a submission checklist.</p><h3 id="chapter-10-—-open-problems" tabindex="-1">Chapter 10 — Open problems <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-10-—-open-problems" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 10 — Open problems&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Constructing questions that genuinely require the whole video; theory of bounded state; how cheap &quot;look again&quot; should be; representing time; causality and counterfactuals; multi-video and lifelong understanding; standardising the cost–accuracy Pareto front; event-level audio-visual alignment; mechanistic accounts of hallucination; the understanding–generation loop; and a research-opportunity heat/crowding table.</p><h3 id="chapters-11-and-12-—-all-1546-papers-one-entry-each" tabindex="-1">Chapters 11 and 12 — All 1546 papers, one entry each <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapters-11-and-12-—-all-1546-papers-one-entry-each" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapters 11 and 12 — All 1546 papers, one entry each&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Chapter 11</strong> covers the <strong>642 papers cited in the body</strong>, four fields each: problem / method / numbers / what to take away.</li><li><strong>Chapter 12</strong> covers the <strong>904 papers that appear only in Appendix A</strong>, two fields each, grouped into the same 20 themes as the appendix.</li><li>Numbers in every entry are transcribed verbatim from the arXiv abstract; entries say so explicitly when the abstract gives none.</li></ul><h3 id="appendices" tabindex="-1">Appendices <a class="header-anchor" href="#appendices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Appendices&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A — the full 1456-paper list by theme with a monthly distribution table; B — glossary; C — learning paths; D — open-source resources.</p><h2 id="navigation" tabindex="-1">Navigation <a class="header-anchor" href="#navigation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Navigation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every arXiv link in the body and in Appendix A is followed by a superscript <sup>§</sup> that jumps to that paper&#39;s entry in Chapter 11 or 12; each entry&#39;s <code>arXiv:ID</code> links back out to the paper. There are <strong>2269 such jump links and 1546 anchors</strong>, so any paper mentioned anywhere in the tutorial is one click from its breakdown.</p>',52)])])}const g=a(o,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
